A||||3||jmw||Jacks, Tires and Wheels||||5||
Z||000000||jmw||09-05-2000||08:43 PM||||Hi. Can anyone tell me if the jack and wrench that come with the Fiero are specific to the car? As in, are they identified by part numbers? I am like 99.9% positive my mechanic took mine out of my Fiero when I last had it in for repair. I did not realize this until I got a flat over the weekend. Am going to ask Mr. Mechanic if he still has my stuff, but want to make sure I get mine (and not somebody else's) back, if I get one at all.<P>As for tires, I've had my Fiero since '84 and have always had 215/60/14s on it (Eagle GTs for the most part). I went to tirerack.com and it said that 185/80/13s are standard on it. [I'm like, "huh?" I have never heard this.] I used to drive my Fiero daily, but am now only driving for pleasure. What tires (brand, size, rating, etc.) would you all recommend? I do not have a lot of money, but want what's best for the car. I live in the South and would not drive in the snow anyway, but would eventually like to be able to take my Fiero on trips. [It has always fishtailed like crazy, even in heavy rain. When I lived up in Michigan I used to drive around with two boat anchors in the trunk just to keep the tires on the road]. <P>I am also in the process of stripping my wheels. The old clearcoat is coming off fine, however there are still large, darker patches of "oxidation". Is there any way to get rid of these so the wheels are uniform in color? I have seen some things about "pitting", etc. but do not have any of that - just the discoloration. Was thinking when I got the clearcoat off and "sanded" them (am using fine steel wool) that this would disappear, but it hasn't.<P>As always, any advice would be appreciated. Sorry this is so long. I have more questions, but will save for another day / post. Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||09-05-2000||11:35 PM||||The 13" tires are for the standard 13" steel wheels that came on the base Fiero. You have an SE which came with the 14" wheels, Hence the 215/60R14 tires. The brand and size I would recommend would be:<BR>BFGoodrich Radial T/A's in 215/60R14. They are very nice tires without spending a large amount of cash. Average is $60-$70 per tire and occasionally you can even get coupons to local stores under the "Buy 3 get 1 free" deal. the BFG's are a much better tire than the Eagle GT's. The first time you drive in the rain you'll agree.<BR> As for removing the staining, have you tried "Mothers Aluminum polish"? You could also try sanding the area with 220 grit sand paper. Then used 400, then 600 grit to smooth it out. I used these to clean up a set of GT wheels.  You could probably go over the entire wheel with the 600 grit then polish with the "Mothers" for a uniform finish and color.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 09-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||09-05-2000||11:37 PM||||On the tire thing.. base coupes and sport coupes came standard with steel wheel's that came with 185/80/13's, SE's came standard with the alloy wheel, and it was an option on the sport coupe.  They came with 215/60/14 wheels.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||09-06-2000||05:12 PM||||I agree with Orief on tire selection.  I've been using BF Goodrich T/A for years and am very pleased with them.||||reg||1
